  - size_t is just an unsigned variable of the largest size available on your system, to guarantee true portability (see sizeof() earlier in this document)
  - allocating memory for a string is simple; similar to allocating an array, you can simply provide malloc n + 1, which allows for a '\0' character after:

      char *p;
      p = malloc(n + 1);

  - void * pointers are automatically converted to the type you're working with; even still, you can use (char *) to explicitly convert (especially nice for human readers)
  - malloc() does not clear nor initialize memory; it may well contain old information! strncpy() is one way to initialize

"17.3 Dynamically Allocated Arrays"

  - The sizeof() operator is required to properly determine the size of the array:

