      a = malloc(n * sizeof(int));

  - Once a points to a dynamic block of memory, it can be treated like an array, and referred to simply by name[] or name as needed.

  "The calloc function"
  - calloc() is simillar to malloc(), but ensures all bits are set to 0.

  - calloc() takes two arguments: 1. the number of elements; 2. the size of the elements. you can free up enough space for any single variable with calloc():

      struct point (int x, y; } *p;
      p = calloc(1, sizeof(struct point));

  "The realloc function"
  - Used to reallocate an existing array that needs to be resized. it's prototype:

      void *realloc(void *ptr, size_t size;

  - ptr is a pointer to a previous call of malloc, calloc, or realloc; size is the new size of the block.
  - realloc() will generally not destroy the data contained within; even if the whole thing must be moved to a new address, realloc() will copy the actual contents as well, preserving them (in theory!)
  - Be sure to update all pointers, since realloc() may well have moved your block elsewhere (and double-check the content is still there maybe!)

"17.4 Deallocating Storage"

  - memory allocation functions obtain memory blocks from a storage pool known as the "heap"
  - Calling these functions too often can exhaust the heap, causing functions to return a null pointer!
  - Programs may even allocate memory, then lose track of them! This is called a "memory leak":

      p = malloc(...);
      q = malloc(...);
      p = q;
      // Notice how the original p allocation is lost forever? It will remain there until the program crashes, very bad!

  "The free function"
  - C has no automatic garbage collection...you need to put on your big boy/girl pants and do it yourself. The free() function exists for exactly this!

      p = malloc(...);
      q = malloc(...);
      free(p);
      p = q;
      // Notice how we free p and set it loose before we lose sight of it!

  - It's legal to send free() a Null pointer; it just will not have any effect

  "The 'Dangling bointer' Problem"

  - The above call of free(p) deallocates the memory block, but doesn't change p itself
  - You *must* either destroy or stop utilizing a dangling pointer once a block is freed!

