      for (p = first; p != NULL; p=p->next) {}

  "Deleting a Node from a Linked List"

    "1" Locate the node to be deleted
      for (cur = list, prev = NULL; cur != NULL && cur->value != n; prev = cur, cur = cur->next) {}

    "2" Alter the previous node so it "bypasses" the deleted node.
      prev->next = cur->next;

    "3" Call free() to reclaim the space occupied by the deleted node
      free(cur);

  - Locating the node is harder than it looks, since once you locate the node, you need to know the *previous* node, so you can rebuild the chain
  - The "trailing pointer" is one way:

      for (cur = list, prev = NULL; cur != NULL && cur->value != n; prev = cur, cur = cur->next) {}

Note: There is no body in this for loop. Just an example of how powerful for() can be!

  - Perform the bypass required above in step 2, making the pointer in the previous node point to the node after the current:

      prev->next = cur->next;

  "Ordered Lists"
  - Similar to a Linked List, but with the nodes sorted by the data inside the nodes, instead of their original order.

