  - If p points to an object that is later modified, then that object cannot be accessed in any way other than through p
  - Having multiple ways to access an object is called "aliasing"
  - If a restricted variable is declared as a local variable without "extern" storage class, restrict applies only to p when the block p is  declared is executed (aka body of a function)
  - restrict allows programmers to see that variables are not meant to overlap, and helps to explain the behavior of certain built-in functions that, before C99, couldn't warn you of the danger
