"17.8 Flexible Array Members (C99)"
  - Sometimes you don't know how large you want an array to be; so, traditionally, programmers would carry out "the struct hack":

    struct vstring {
      int len;
      char chars[1];
    };
    struct vstring *str = malloc(sizeof(struct vstring) +n -1);
    str->len = n;

  - You set the length of chars to 1, that way you can just manually allocate as much as you want for each string of n length 
  - This procedure has become so popular, it actually made it into C99. Now, you declare the array without a size, like so:

      struct vstring {
        int len;
        char chars[]; 	/* Note how the length has been omitted...C99 automatically turns this into a "flexible array member" upon creation!
      };

  - This works because C99 will simply ignore the size of the chars[] array when sizing up the struct, which means you will get exact n characters, and do not need to subtract for the array
  - "Special rules": FAM must appear last in the struct, and struct must have 1+ other members. Copying a struct with an FAM will copy the other members, but *not* the FAM Array!
  - This is considered an "incomplete type", meaning the compiler does not have enough information to determine how much memory it requires (and is thus subject to restrictions)
  - "incomplete type" (or a struct that contains it) cannot be a member of another struct, or an element of an array (an array can have pointers to structs with FAM though!)

