"18.1 Declaration Syntax"
  - A declaration generally appears in the format "declaration-specifiers declarators ;"
  - A "declaration specifier" describes the properties of the variable/function. A "declarator" gives their names and provides additional information about their properties
  - Declaration Specificers fall into three categories:
    * "Storage Classes": auto, static, extern, register (may contain only one storage class per declaration. It should come first)
    * "Type Qualifiers": const, volatile, restrict (may contain 0 or more qualifiers)
    * "Type Specifiers": void, char, short, int, long, float, double, signed, unsigned, etc (may be combined)
        Note: structs and typedefs are specifiers, as well
    * "Function Specifiers": inline (used only in function declarations in C99)
  - "initializer": the content used to provide an initial value (int n = 10 would have an initializer of 10)

"18.2 Storage Classes"
  - "Storage Duration": determines when memory is set aside. a "static" variable stays at the same memory location as long as the program is running
  - "Linkage": a variable with "external linkage" can be shared by several files in a program; "internal linkage" is limited to functions in same file; no linkage restricted to single function
    * variables declared outside of functions (such as "global scope" variables) will have "external linkage". Using "static" will make that same global variable now limited to the same file
  - "Static Variable values are shared between recursive functions!"
  - variables in an "extern" declaration always have static duration

  "The register Storage Class"
  - using the register storage class asks the compiler to store the variable in the CPU register instead of in memory. this is a request, not a command!
  - legal only for variables in a block. Shares all properties of an auto variable, *except* "you cannot use &, as it lacks an address (even if the compiler puts it in memory!)

  "The Storage Class of a Function"
  - May only use extern and static
  - "extern": specifies the function has external linkage (and may be called from other files). This is the default
  - "static": indicates internal linkage, and can only be called within the file in which it's defined.
  - "name space pollution": occurs when the same function name is used in multiple files, which can cause issues when external. "static" reduces the impact of this

"18.3 Type Qualifiers"
  - two types: "const" and "volatile" (the latter is low-level programming only)
  - In embedded systems, const identifies memory to be stored in ROM
  - const differs from using a declare statement in a few key ways:
    * const provides context to viewers of your source code, explains what the functions are supposed to do (And shows which variables will not change)
    * const cannot be used in arrays, such as const int n; array[n] = x;
    * declare is pre-compiler only, so anything that must occur at runtime should use const

"18.4 Declarations"
  - array brackets can be left empty if the array is a parameter, has an initializer, or if its storage class is "extern" (since we won't know its size yet)
  - C99 allows the use of "static" to declare an array's minimum length, like: static array[5], or an asterisk to denote variable-length: array[*]
  - C allows parameter names to be omitted. Even parentheses can be empty! Note: this is VERY old (pre-C89), don't use it. modern prototypes kick butt comparatively
  - 

"18.5 Initializers"
  - the equals sign (=) is an initializer; however, much like an asterisk (*) is different during declaration, so is the equals. It's not the same as declaration!
  - the initializer for a "static" variable must be constant! You can use define macros, but *not* variables!
  - the same is true of arrays of *all kinds* in C89; however, in C99, you can use variables for arrays as long as they aren't static
  - the initializer for an auto struct or union can be another struct or union

  "Uninitialized Variables"
  - the initial value of a variable depends on its storage duration, it is NOT always undefined!
  - variables with "auto" storage duration have "no default value"
  - variables with "static" storage durationhave "default value of zero". It is correctly initialized by type, meaning "a static float will be 0.0, not 0"
  - As a matter of style (and perhaps good taste), it's better to always provide an initializer for static variables

"18.6 Inline Functions (C99)"
  - C99 has the keyword "inline", which relates to machine instructions for a function
  - C *always* copies its functions by value (which is why you need pointers to make changes to objects passed into functions)
  - this, and the work to return the function, are collectively called "overhead". C99's "inline" keyword is aimed at "reducing function overhead"
  - using "inline", every call of the function is replaced by the machine instructions for that function. It reduces overhead, but may increase size
  - inline does not actually force the compiler to use anything specific though; it simply explains that it should make these calls as fast as possible
  - like register and restrict, the compiler is free to ignore inline. These days, compilers are so fast, it's probably only *rarely* even beneficial (and only slightly)
  
  "Inline Definitions"
  - if the standard call of a function has external linkage (can be called elsewhere), adding inline will break that functionality. It will cause an error
  - adding "static" explicitly claims "internal linkage" so it can't be called. There is another way, but it is DIRTY: make a second prototype for the function in another file
  - adding a second definition of a function is not recommended because you can't guarantee they will remain consistent when the function is modified


