"19.1 Modules"
  - a module is a collection of services, some of which are made available to other parts of the program ("clients")
  - "Abstraction" means that a properly-designed program allows the users to not know what some of the functions even do. You only need know the inputs and outputs!
    *NOTE: I personally feel that, over long periods of time, this has culminated in many programmers not understanding the programs at a fundamental level...which is why I learned C!

"Cohesion and Coupling"
  - A well-design program will have:
    * "High Cohesion": elements of each module should be related to one another, with a common goal
    * "Low Coupling": Modules should be as independent as possible from each other. Makes it easier to reuse modules and modify programs

"Types of Modules"
  - "Data Pool": a collection of related variables/constants. In C, is often just a header file (for constants, not variables!)
  - "Library": a collection of related functions. They may be created by C, or they may be created by users. Again, you only need know the inputs and outputs to use most of the time!
  - "Abstract Object" A collection of functions operating on a hidden data structure (object = collection of data bundled with operations on said data)
  - "Abstract Data Type (ADT)": a type whose representation is hidden (see Section 19.5 for more on information hiding)

"19.2 Information Hiding"
  - modules keep some information secret. As long as the inputs and outputs are clear, the inner workings can be left mysterious. This is done for both "security" and "flexibility"
  - "static" storage class shines at information hiding. file-level variables with static have internal linkage, anbd cannot be accessed by clients of the module in question

"19.3 Abstract Data Types"
  - "typedef" is used to create abstract data types; for instance, to have multiple Structs (I feel you could just accomplish this by making two structs but, ok)
  - The idea is that clients do not know (or need to know) what underlies the type. for instance, "stack" could be a struct, or it could be an array, but it doesn't matter to the client
  - C is not well-equipped for "encapsulation", aka the process of truly hiding what data types are being used underneath a type, and they can still be called as such

  "Incomplete Type"
  - The only tool for "encapulation" provided by C is the incomplete type, a type that describes objects but lacks the information needed to determine the size of said object

      struct t; //example of incomplete type (which we presume has been elucidated upon elsewhere, to describe the members of the struct)

  - You cannot create a variable of that struct type; however, you can create pointers to that variable, which will (eventually) be joined to an actual struct of that type

"19.5 Design Issues for Abtract Data Types (ADTs)"

  "Error Handling"
  - The C standard library containers a parameterized macro named "assert" that can terminate a program if specific conditions are not met. This could replace "if" statements

"Q&A"
  - The void type is technically an incomplete data type, and can *never* be completed. It's actually impossible to declare a void variable of this type for that reason
  - sizeof() cannot be applied to an incomplete type!


