"20.1 Bitwise Operators"

  - There are 6 bitwise operators in C that operate on integer data at the bit level: two shift operators, complement, and, exclusive or, and inclusive or

  "Bitwise Shift Operators"
  - Transforms the binary representation of an integer by shifting its bits to the left ("<< left shift") or right ("<< right shift")
  - May be any integer or char type, integer promotions are performed on both operands, and the result has the type of the left operand after promotion
  - The value of i <<j is the result of the bits in i being shifted left by j places. For each shift off the left of i, a zero bit enters the right
  - in >>, as long as i is nonnegative, zeros are added to the left for each shift as needed
  - Some implementations handle negative numbers by adding zeros, others will add 1s, so it's very important to be careful to keep this portable by avoiding signed integers
  - Assuming 16-bit integers, this is how the following operations will look:

    i = 13;			// 0000000000001101
    j = i << 2;    	// 0000000000110100
    j = i >> 2;    	// 0000000000000011

  - "Bitwise operators are essentially working on binary representations of integers"
  - adding an equals sign can cause assignment to occur: >>= (bear in mind these operators have low precedence, so >>= 2 + 1 is like saying >>= (2+1)

  "Bitwise Complement, And, Exclusive Or, and Inclusive Or"
  - The remaining four bitwise operators:

	(IN PRECEDENCE ORDER!)
    ~	bitwise not (complement)		0 replaced with 1, 1 replaced with 0
	&	bitwise and						Produces a 1 when both operands have a 1 (Boolean And)
	^	bitwise exclusive or			Produces a 1 when one operand has a 1, a 0 when they both have a 1, and a 0 when they both have 0 (Boolean or)
	|	bitwise inclusive or			Produces a 1 when one operand has a 1, a 1 when they both have a 1, and a 0 when they both have 0 (Boolean or)

  - the ~ is unary, meaning promotions occur on its own operand. the rest are binary
  - They perform boolean operations on all bits in their operands
  - ^ / | are basically produce a 1 when only one of the operands has a 1
    * "inclusive" means it still produces a 1 when they both do
    * "exclusive" means ONLY one of the two can be 1
  - The ~ operator helps with portability. It create an integer with all 1 bits, which works the same on all sizes (regardless of whether it's a 16- or a 64-bit!)

 "Using the Bitwise Operators to Access Bits"
  - We will often need to store info as single bits, or collections of bits. In Graphics programming, two or more pixels may be squeezed into a single byte
  - Bitwise operators help to extract or modify data associated with small amounts of space, such as altering that single byte via a few bits

  - "Setting a bit"
    * Remember "most significant" bit is the left-most, "least significant" is the right
    * Say you want to set bit 4 of i. You can use a "mask" that has that bit set, such as 0x010:

      i  = 0x0000;	// (0x0000 = 00 = 0000)	 i = 0000000000000000
      i |= 0x0010;	// (0x0010 = 16 = 1000)	 i = 0000000000001000

  - "Clearing a bit" and "Testing a bit" are same as Setting, but use different operators. I'll move on to actual exampels of their use:

    #define BLUE 	1
    #define GREEN 	2
    #define RED		4
    
    i |= BLUE;				// sets BLUE bit
	i &= ~BLUE;	 			// clears BLUE bit
	if (i & BLUE)			// Tests BLUE bit
    i ^= BLUE; 				// flips BLUE bit
   
    i |= BLUE | GREEN; 		// sets BLUE and GREEN bits
    i &= ~(BLUE | GREEN); 	// clears BLUE and GREEN bits
    if (i & BLUE | GREEN)) 	// tests whether BLUE or GREEN bits are set

  "Using Bitwise Operators to Access Bit-Fields"
  - Bitwise and clears the bit-field, bitwise or stores new bits in the field
  - Let's look at an example that stores 101 in bits 4-6, and does not touch the rest:
    * i = i & ~0x0070 | 0x0050; // 
      - ~0x0070 = ~0000 0000 0111 0000 = 1111 1111 1000 1111
    * i & 1111 1111 1000 1111 | 0x0050
      -  0x0050  =  0000 0000 0101 0000
    * i = xxxx xxxx x000 xxxx | 0000 0000 101 0000
    * i = xxxx xxxx x101 xxxx
    * x's are used for values for which we cannot predict the final value;
    * the & guarantees that the x bits are unchanged, since we have 1s for all those values, and the | brings over anything that is a 1 from the old value

"20.2 Bit-fields in Structures"

  - Structs can be used to take the cumbersome, tricky concepts of 20.1, and package them into something more usable
  - MS-DOS would split dates into a single integer, since they only needed four bits for the month (0-16 is plenty), etc
  - C "structs have a special feature for bit-fields", which "uses a : character":

    struct file_date {
      unsigned int day: 5;
      unsigned int month: 4;
      unsigned int year: 7;
    };

  - The number after each is the length (in bits) of each member. You could condence these statements, into 'unsigned int day: 5, month: 4, year: 7', but that's tacky
  - Must be int, unsigned int, or signed int. "bit-fields MUST be integers!"

  "How Bit-Fields Are Stored"
  - C gives the compiler a lot of latitude in determining how to choose a bit-field
  - Generally stored as "storage units" in values of 8 bits, 16 bits, or 32 bits
  - Bit-field names are entirely optional in C, but are useful as padding to ensure other bit fields are properly positioned

"20.3 Other Low-Level Techniques"

  "Defining Machine-Dependent Types"
  - Since char only occupies one byte, it can be used to create a typedef for byte

    typedef unsigned char BYTE;

  - Other types can be used this way, as well, such as 16-bit words (unsigned short WORD)

  "Using Unions to Provide Multiple Views of Data"
  - Unions are often used to view a block of members in different ways:

    union int_date {
      unsigned short i;
      struct file_date fd;
    };

  - This allows us to view the same block of memory as either a simple short integer of two bytes, or as a struct of two bytes designed to show the date
  - Works great with registers, which are divided into smaller units
    * Modern CPUs may be different in this regard, but older x86 have 16-bit registers AX, BX, CX, DX etc, further divided into AH (high) and AL (low), both 8-bits
    * Setting up two structs allows the register to either be worked on as BH and BL, or as the entirety of BX
  - "Big-Endian (natural order)": the left-most byte is stored first; "little-endian": the right-most byte is stored first. "x85 is little-endian!"

  "Using Pointers as Addresses"
  - Pointer addresses often have the same bits as an integer, so you can cast an int into a pointer, which can serve as the address:

    BYTE *p;				// typedef for unsigned char
    p = (BYTE *) 0x1000;	// p contains a pointer to the memory address 0x1000 now

  - Most programs are only allowed to run in "protected mode", which means you cannot access any memory not allocated by or for the program (prevents hacks, crashes, and fun)
  - The main function itself receives a memory address, which can be viewed by a user!


