"21.1 Using the library"

  - There are 15 C89, 24 C99 libraries. Compilers often come with more, but they are non-standard (most C compilers have been around forever, they're prob easy to identify)
  - Using a header means you can't re-use any of its function names for your own. Names with file scope cannot be redefined at file level (like <stdio.h> and size_t)
  - Identifiers starting with an underscore followed by an upper-case letter (or second underscore) are reserved. You should never use them
  - Every identifier with external linkage in the std library is reserved, even if you don't include it in your program!_

 "Functions Hidden by Macros"
  - Many programmers replace small functions with parameterized macros, even in the standard library. C allows for this, but proteects you by requiring a true function is also available
  - This results in many library headers declaring both a macro *and* a function with the same name:

      int getchar(void);
      \#define getchar() getc(stdin) // getchar() defined as getc macro

  - When the real function is needed, "the #undef directive will ensure the true function is run, not the macro"

