"22.1 Streams"

  - A stream is any input or output source/destination. stdout would be a stream, as would a file, or stdin, or even cds/dvds, printers, anything

  "File Pointers"
  - file pointers have the type "FILE *", and are declared as needed. Say you need two streams:

    FILE *fp1, *fp2;

  -stdio.h has three streams (yes, those ones!):
   *stdin
   *stdout
   *stderr
  - By default, the keyboard represents stdin, and the screen stdout, but "input redirection" changes this: demo < in.dat
  - "output redirection" is the same: demo > out.dat
  - They can even be combined: demo < in.dat > out.dat (reads from in.dat, and sends output to out.dat)

  - When copying a file, and you aren't sure whether it is text or binary, assume it is binary! Otherwise important EOF data may be lost

"22.2 File Operations"

  "Opening a file"

    FILE *fopen (const char * restrict filename, const char * restrict mode);

  - "fopen" will open a stream from a file. The first argument is the file to be opened, and the second is the mode (read, write, etc)
  - "restrict" is a keyword in c that prevents any other pointer from opening that same memory location for its entire lifetime
  - always "test the return value of fopen to be sure it's not a null pointer"!

  "Closing a file"

    FILE *fclose(FILE *stream);

  - fclose's argument must be a file pointer from a call of fopen or freopen. fclose returns zero if the file was closed successfully; otherwise, returns EOF (a macro defined in <stdio.h>)

  "Attaching a File to an Open Stream"

    FILE *freopen (const char * restrict filename, const char * restrict mode, FILE * restrict stream);

  - freopen attaches a different file to a stream you've already opened. Commonly used to associate a file with stdin/stdout/stderr
  - if freopen can open the file, it will return the pointer you provided; otherwise, it returns a null pointer
  - C99: even if it fails, freopen will still try to change the stream's mode to that provided by the command. no guarantees!

  "Obtaining File Names from the Command Line"
  
    int main(int argc, char *argv[])

  - argc = argument count, argv = argument vector
  - argv[0] is the program name (aka the first argument) argv[argc - 1] is the last argumentargv[argc] is a NULL pointer

  "Temporary Files"
  - Programmers often create temp files that exist only while the program runs. Think of it like a way of using disk space instead of memory
  - "tmpfile" creates a temporary file (in "wb+" mode, and returns a file pointer to access the file during the program:

    FILE *tempptr;
    tempptr = tmpfile();

  - unfortunately, we don't know the name of the file, and it cannot later be made permanent. To do that, we need fopen, but we need to be absolutely *sure* the filename we choose is not in use
  - "tmpnam" is for naming temporary files. It provides a unique name so you are not accidentally overwriting files that exist
  - if a null pointer is provided, it stores the filename in a static variable and returns a pointer. otehrwise, it copies the filename into a character array you provide:

    char *filename;
    filename = tmpnam(NULL);
    ---------
    char filename[L_tmpnam]; 	// L_tmpnam is an <stdio.h> macro specifying how long to make the char array that holds the temporary filename
    tmpnam(filename); 			// returns a pointer to the first char in the array when successful

  - Use sparingly, as TMP_MAX macro is the limit of the number of open files you can have. Always look for a null pointer!

  "File Buffering"

    int fflush(FILE *stream);
    void setbuf(FILE * restrict stream, char * restrict buf);
	int setvbuf(FILE * restrict stream, char * restrict buf, int mode, size_t size);

  - Transfers to and from disks are pretty slow (well, not so bad these days, but you get the idea). "buffering" solves this by storing data written to a stream in a buffer area in memory
  - When full or a stream is closed, it is flushed via fflush (aka, written to the output device and cleared from memory)
  - C does a lot of this for you, automatically...but sometimes, you need to take matters in your own hands, and as usual, C will let you do it!
  - Calling fflush(NULL) will flush all streams
  - "setvbuf" changes the size and location of the buffer, and how buffering happens (via the int mode parameter)"

    _IOFBF	full buffering		data is read when stream is empty or buffer is full (this works with both input and output!)
	_IOLBF	line buffering 		data is read one line at a time
	_IONBF	no buffering		data is read immediately to the stream, with no buffer file
  - buf is the address of the buffer. auto storage duration will be reclaimed at block exit; dynamic allows us to choose when to reclaim
  - size is the number of bites in the buffer (space vs performance)

    char buffer[N];
	setvbuf(stream, buffer, _IOFBF, N); // sets stream to buffer to buffer[] array, in Full Buffer mode, size N

  - setvbuf must be called after stream is open, but before any operations are performed on it
  - "setbuf" is like vbuf, but not variable, and it presumes the size and mode. basically, don't use it

  "Miscellaneous File Operations"
  
    int remove(const char *filename);
    int rename(const char *old, const char *new);

  - These two work on the filename itself, not a pointer to the memory location. They return zero if they succeed, and non-zero if they fail
  - Make sure you close a file before you remove it. This applies to files opened with fopen. Removing a currently-open file is implementation-defined and thus unpredictable sometimes

"22.3 Formatted I/O"

  - "fprintf" is like printf, but it writes to the stream indicated in the first argument
  - often used to print to stderr

  - in printf, you can actually use * as a variable to enter the value as a function parameter, ie "%*.4d", 6, i);
  - this allows for use of a macro, as well
  - %n allows you to store the number of characters read by printf into a &variable
  - "scanf" returns the number of objects it successfully read:

    while (scanf("%d", &i) == 1) {} //Ensures you have read a real value each time



