  - Output Functions: fputc, putc, putchar
  - "putchar" writes one character to stdout
  - "fputc" and "putc" are like getchar, but let you choose a stream. putc is generally impleemnted as a macro as well as a function
  - Input Functions: fgetc, getc, getchar, ungetc

      while ((ch = getc(fp)) != EOF) {} // Idiom for pulling in text from a stream

  - Always use Int to store the return variable for getc, fgetc, or getchar when using EOF, as char can give the wrong result
  - "ungetc" pushes back a character from a stream and clears the stream's EOF indicator
  - 


"22.5 Line I/O"

    int fputs(const char * restrict s, FILE * restrict stream);
    int puts(const char *s);

  - As a rule of thumb, do not use any function that does not provide the size or count to traverse. aka, use fgets instead of gets, and fput instead of put
  - for scanf, you can use scanf("%N" which I believe will tell how many characters to read in
  - I took no further notes here as these are very basic puts/gets commands, and will likely be read in-depth more during string.h

"22.6 Block I/O"

    size_t fread(void * restrict ptr, size_t size, size_t nmemb, FILE * restrict stream);
    size_t fwrite( constvoid * restrict ptr, size_t size, size_t nmemb, FILE * restrict stream);

  - used for large blocks of data, and *generally* limited to binary streams, though you can use it with text if you are really careful
  - "fwrite" copies an array from memory to a stream. Function takes: array address, size of each array element, number of elements, and a file pointer

    fwrite(a, sizeof(a[0]), sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0]), fp);

  - "fwrite returns the number of elements written
  - "fread" reads elements of an array from a stream. Function takes: array address, size of each array element, number of elements, and a file pointer

    n = fread(a, sizeof(a[0]), sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0]), fp);

  - The return value should equal the third argument, unless end of the file or some error occurred. feof and ferror can be used to determine this
  - fwrite is mostly used to store data to a file before a program terminates; later, when the program restarts, fread can pull the data base into memory
  - doesn't HAVE to be an array, it can work with all types of variables. Structs, for example:

    fwrite(&s, sizeof(s), 1, fp);

  - Remember that pointers from, for example, array members read into fwrite/fread are not guaranteed to be valid when they come back in


"22.6 File Positioning"

    int fgetpos(FILE * restrict stream, fpos_t * restrict pos);
    int fseek(FILE *stream, long int offset, int whence);
    int fsetpos(FILE *stream, const fpos_t * pos);
    long int ftell(FILE *stream);
    void rewind(FILE *stream);

  - Streams have "file positions". By default, they are set at the beginning of a file, unless in append mode
  - If you don't want to start at the beginning (or first free space, for append), you need file positioning functions
  - "fseek" changes the file position associated with the first arg. Second arg is a (possibly negative) byte count. Third argument specifies what it's calculated in comparison to:
    * SEEK_SET	Beginning of file
    * SEEK_CIR	Current file position
	* SEEK_END	End of file
  - To move backwards ten bytes from current position:

    fseek(fp, -10L, SEEK_CUR);

  - fseek returns a nonzero value when it fails
  - TEXT STREAMS ONLY: either offset must be zero, or whence must be SEEK_SET and offset a value obtained by a call of ftell
    * AKA, you can only move to the beginning or end of a text stream, or visit a previously-visited place
  - "ftell" returns the current file position as a long int
  - If an error occurs, it returns -1L and stores an error code in errno
  - the second argument in fseek is generally a file position saved from an ftell() call
  - If working with a text stream, ftell isn't necessarily a byte count, so you want to be careful not to perform arithmetic on ftell return values
  - "rewind" sets a file position back at the beginning. It's only different from fseek 0/SEEK_SET in that it returns nothing and clears the error indicator
  - "fgetpos" and "fsetpos" are for very large files, since they use type fpos_t instead of long int

"22.8 String I/O"

    int sprintf(char * restrict s, const char * restrict format, ...);
    int snprintf(char * restrict s, size_t n, const char * restrict format, ...);

  - "sprintf" writes output into a char array[], pointed to by its first argument, instead of a stream. the second arg is a format string like printf uses
  
    sprintf(date, "%d/%d/%d", 9, 20, 2010);

  - if there are encoding errors, sprintf returns a negative value
  - "snprintf" same as printf, but ensures no more than n-1 chars are written (-1 because of the \0 at the end, which is always written unless n is zero)

    int sscanf(char * restrict s, const char * restrict format, ...);

  - "sscanf" is like scanf, but reads from a string. Allows us to examine an input time as many times as we need, since we don't have to write it
  - sscanf returns the number of data items successfully read and stored. returns EOF if it reached the end of a string (\0) before finding the first item

"Q & A"
  - Binary is usually preferable to text if storing both characters and numbers. Numbers are in binary form in memory, so copying is easy. In text form they're mcuh slower
  - Binary files are both hard for humans read, and also not generally portable between Operating Systems (variable sizes, in particular, can be different on different OSes)
  - if abort() is used to terminate a program, files are not always closed, and it's best practice to use fclose to ensure they are closed!
  - Format strings for scanf can be variables. K&R C has the following awesome idiom:

    while (--argc > 0) {
	    printf((argc > 1) ? "%s " : "%s", *++argv); // evaluates to "%s " for all arguments but the last, which ensures it receives no space after!
    }
  - Another cool idiom is to make scanf skip all characters up to a newline using the *[] feature:

    scanf("%*[^\n]"); // it's not being stored anywhere, so it just deletes the input stream!

  - fflush() is designed for *output* streams...using it with input streams is undefined and should never be done!
